<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tacitus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 56–c. 120</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tacitus was a Roman historian and senator of the late first and early second centuries CE, author of the Annals, the Histories and other works. His writings are a major source for the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> and its frontiers. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garamantian-empire">Garamantian Empire</a> note that Tacitus, writing around 109 CE, still described the Garamantes of the Libyan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> as unconquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tacitus.jpg" alt="Tacitus" width="318" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pe-Jo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wien-_Parlament-Tacitus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Roman period the kingdom fought Scythians, Sarmatians and Tauri, often with Roman help, and during the war of 45–49 CE Roman troops and their allies stormed the town of Uspe, an episode described by <b>Tacitus</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bosporan-kingdom">Bosporan Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>From the second century BCE it sided with Rome, and according to <b>Tacitus</b>, envoys reminded the Senate in 53 CE of the help their city had given to Roman commanders in wars against the Macedonian kings, Antiochus III, Aristonicus, the pirates and Mithridates VI of Pontus; Claudius responded…</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…met to honour the emperor; in 15 CE Tiberius allowed the province to build a temple to the deified Augustus there, which <b>Tacitus</b> says set an example for all the provinces, and the upper city was later rebuilt as a monumental complex for the provincial cult and council.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hispania-tarraconensis">Hispania Tarraconensis · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Svear of the Mälaren valley and Uppland, centred on the royal and cult site of Uppsala, appear in sources from <b>Tacitus</b> onward, while the Geats lived in Västergötland and Östergötland to the south-west.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 69 CE some 9,000 Roxolani raiders were caught by the Legio III Gallica in Moesia on ground softened by a thaw, and <b>Tacitus</b> describes armoured riders unable to rise once unhorsed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sarmatians">Sarmatians · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Greek and Roman writers, among them Caesar, Cicero, <b>Tacitus</b> and Pliny the Elder, describe the druids as magico-religious specialists held in high esteem in Gaul and Britain.</blockquote><a href="/religion/celtic-religion">Celtic religion · Community &amp; Society</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-narbonensis">Gallia Narbonensis</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44,4&amp;year=97" aria-label="Gallia Narbonensis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4646 605 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Tacitus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5096 875h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5096 875h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44,4&amp;year=97">What was here in 97 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;And now bills were passed, not only for national objects but for individual cases, and laws were most numerous when the commonwealth was most corrupt.&#8221;</p><footer>Annals, book 3 &#183; tr. A. J. Church and W. J. Brodribb, 1876 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Annals_(Tacitus)/Book_3" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…without either bitterness or partiality, from any motives to which I am far removed.&#8221;</p><footer>Annals, book 1 &#183; tr. A. J. Church and W. J. Brodribb, 1876 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Annals_(Tacitus)/Book_1" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…for it is the rare fortune of these days that a man may think what he likes and say what he thinks.&#8221;</p><footer>Histories, book 1 &#183; tr. W. Hamilton Fyfe, 1912 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16927/pg16927-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;When he was a commoner he seemed too big for his station, and had he never been emperor, no one would have doubted his ability to reign.&#8221;</p><footer>Histories, book 1 &#183; tr. W. Hamilton Fyfe, 1912 &#183; of the emperor Galba, after his murder in 69 CE &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16927/pg16927-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Indeed never has it been proved by such terrible disasters to Rome or by such clear evidence that Providence is concerned not with our peace of mind but rather with vengeance for our sin.&#8221;</p><footer>Histories, book 1 &#183; tr. W. Hamilton Fyfe, 1912 &#183; of the year of the four emperors and the civil wars that followed &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16927/pg16927-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;All this in their ignorance they called civilisation, when it was but a part of their servitude.&#8221;</p><footer>Agricola, 21 &#183; tr. A. J. Church and W. J. Brodribb, 1877 &#183; of the Britons taking up Roman dress, baths and banquets &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/ancient/tacitus-agricola.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
